; TEST: emit-exit=4
;
; `vadd f64 %a %b`: the vector group's opcode spelled with a SCALAR type.
; The verifier passes it -- SH_BIN's rule is operand agreement, and two
; f64 operands with an f64 result agree, the way fma's d_trivial passes
; in reject_emit_fma.ir; the EMITTER refuses it by name --
; "vadd/vsub/vmul/vdiv: the type must be a vector float
; (`vf32.N`/`vf64.N`, N = 2, 4 or 8)" -- because one packed op over one
; lane is the scalar group misspelt (fadd is that instruction), and a
; silent scalar fallback would hide the spelling error. Exit 4 here, not
; 5, is the evidence the two layers disagree on purpose -- the same
; split reject_emit_fma.ir records for the missing fused op.
;
; This fixture is the refusal-set parity anchor for the C twin: the C
; emitter MUST refuse this same text with this same wording. That its twin
; does so is asserted here, not observed -- backend_c is another tree and
; this wave does not touch it; the differential phase is where parity gets
; proven, so the claim stands [UNTESTED] until that run.
;
; Mutation (run): __bfa_emit_varith's type guard dropped and the f64
; chunked loop run on a zero byte count -> emit_ir exits 0 having emitted
; no arithmetic at all -- the vector "result" is whatever garbage the
; region-3 home happened to hold. This fixture is what keeps that
; mutation loud.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = fconst f64 4609434218613702656
%2 = fconst f64 4611686018427387904
%3 = vadd f64 %1 %2
%4 = iconst u8 0
ret %4
}
